in @prism
in @glass
in @nl
in @mirror/lens
in @mirror/store

# @mirror/lens/unix — the filesystem lens (OS-level impedance).
#
# The ouroboros pipeline's load-bearing impedance layer. mirror's
# substrate truth lives in @mirror/store as content-addressed shards
# (oid-graph closure; per shards/mirror/store.mirror). OS-level
# downstream consumers — cargo, erlc, flang — read source from disk
# as Unix files. The lens IS the impedance match: it presents the
# store's shard contents to those consumers AS a Unix filesystem,
# without materializing a copy.
#
# Per docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md the pipeline is:
#
#   mirror substrate
#     -> @code/macro codegen (shim_prism / shim_action / shim_grammar /
#        shim_type)
#     -> @mirror/store shard (content-addressed; oid-graph closure)
#     -> @mirror/lens/unix (presents shard as Unix filesystem)
#     -> cargo / erlc / flang reads the "files"
#     -> binary
#     -> IS @spectral runtime
#
# Three runtime targets ship with mirror (Rust / Erlang / Fortran via
# @code/rust, @code/erlang, @code/fortran); all three pass through
# THIS lens. The lens is target-agnostic; the cargo / erlc / flang
# distinction is a consumer-side concern. This species declares the
# Unix-shaped impedance surface; the platform sub-species (FUSE on
# Linux, 9P over loopback, NFS, etc.) bind to the host OS.
#
# === Family placement ===
#
# Seventh species under @mirror/lens. Transport-side lens; audience
# is the operating system itself; the surface is a POSIX filesystem
# (paths, read, write, stat); the algebra is unchanged. Per
# docs/specs/the-convergence.md the four transport lenses
# (cli / shell / mcp / lsp) render the algebra for human / agent /
# editor audiences; the unix lens renders it for the OS — the
# audience that is itself a compiler toolchain.
#
# Same Transparency<P> verdict shape as the other lenses; same
# eigenboard-tagged output discipline; the daemon stays the
# regulator (two cargos pointed at the same store MUST resolve the
# same oid for the same path, per the-convergence.md §1.4
# convergence-by-construction).
#
# === Why a lens, not a port ===
#
# The Unix filesystem IS one observation surface onto the same
# five-operation algebra. `focus` over a path is `read`; `project`
# is filtered listing (stat / readdir); `split` is sub-tree
# decomposition (the oid-graph's children become directory entries);
# `shift` is path rewriting (mount-point translation); `settle` is
# the consumer's view of the materialized impedance match. The
# convergence-by-construction property holds: two consumers reading
# the same mounted path MUST receive the same bytes.
#
# === Body status ===
#
# The action surface declares the mount / path / read / write /
# stat shape; bodies (the platform-specific FUSE / 9P / passthrough
# dispatcher) land when the platform sub-species are declared.
#
# v0 floor discharge (Mara, 2026-06-12): the abstract bodies
# discharge through `bootstrap/src/lens_unix.rs`'s `UnixLens`
# struct — a materialize-to-tempdir impedance lens. The
# materialization choice is substrate-pull-correct for v0 because
# the named platform sub-species (FUSE) requires kernel extensions
# on macOS (macFUSE + SIP weakening + reboot) that create a hard
# friction wall for substrate floor work. The abstract action
# surface declared here is implementation-agnostic; future
# `@mirror/lens/unix/fuse` and `@mirror/lens/unix/9p` sub-species
# swap the materialization for a kernel-mediated view without
# changing this declaration.
#
# Action body mapping (v0):
#   mount(shard)              -> UnixLens::mount
#   path(handle, components)  -> UnixLens::path
#   read(p)                   -> UnixLens::read
#   write(p, content)         -> [forward-promised to v1; read-only
#                                 floor is enough for the ouroboros
#                                 pipeline's cargo-reads step]
#   stat(p)                   -> UnixLens::stat
#
# Test: `bootstrap/tests/lens_unix_cargo_roundtrip.rs` verifies
# `cargo metadata --manifest-path <mount>/Cargo.toml` succeeds
# against a Splinter-materialized crate (the closure of substrate
# -> store -> lens -> cargo per docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md §5).
#
# Cascade candidates (forward-promised; not declared this tick):
#
#   @mirror/lens/unix/fuse   FUSE binding (Linux / macOS via
#                            macFUSE). Userspace filesystem
#                            implementation; mount via the kernel's
#                            FUSE module.
#   @mirror/lens/unix/9p     Plan9 9P protocol over loopback. The
#                            cleanest substrate match — 9P's
#                            content-addressed semantics align
#                            with the store's oid-graph; kernel
#                            v9fs mounts the loopback socket.
#
# Additional candidates (smaller surface, possible future ticks):
#   @mirror/lens/unix/nfs     NFSv4 loopback (broadest portability).
#   @mirror/lens/unix/projfs  Windows Projected File System
#                             (cross-platform reach; out of family
#                             root scope this tick).
#
# Platform sub-species commit to the impedance dispatcher; THIS
# family root declares only the impedance shape (the abstract Unix
# surface).
#
# === Substrate-pull placement ===
#
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/mirror/lens/unix.mirror` declares `@mirror/lens/unix` and
# only that. Sub-species at `shards/mirror/lens/unix/<platform>.mirror`
# will declare `@mirror/lens/unix/<platform>` and only that.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md (the ouroboros
#               pipeline; the runtime-target framing this lens
#               closes),
#               docs/specs/the-convergence.md §1.2 (transport lens
#               family discipline; the audience-as-observation
#               framing this lens extends to "OS as audience"),
#               docs/specs/mirror-store.md (the store this lens
#               impedance-matches against).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]],
#                      [[architecture-fragmentation-is-the-rust-substrate]].
# Related shards: lens.mirror (the family root above),
#                 lens/cli.mirror, lens/shell.mirror, lens/mcp.mirror,
#                 lens/lsp.mirror (sibling transport lenses),
#                 lens/transit.mirror, lens/refract.mirror (sibling
#                 measurement lenses),
#                 store.mirror (the carrier; oid, bytes, shard_ref,
#                 splinter_graph).

# === actions — abstract impedance surface; bodies land on
#     platform sub-species pull ===

# mount(shard) — bind a stored shard's oid-graph closure to a
# filesystem handle. The handle is the impedance carrier: through
# it, the shard's content is reachable as Unix paths. Idempotent
# by construction: mounting the same oid twice returns the same
# handle (the handle's identity is a function of the oid).
#
# `imperfect` because the mount can fail with opacity: the oid may
# not resolve in this store (failure(reason)), the platform
# sub-species may be absent (partial(opacity) — no FUSE / 9P
# binding loaded), or the mount-point may already be occupied
# (failure(reason)). On success, carries the filesystem handle.
mount(shard: shard_ref) -> imperfect { \ }

# path(handle, components) — construct a Unix path WITHIN a mounted
# handle's namespace. The components are the substrate-side path
# segments; the lens translates them through the oid-graph's
# directory structure. Pure substrate: no syscall; just a typed
# reference into the mounted namespace.
path(handle: ref, components: ref) -> ref { \ }

# read(p) — fetch bytes at a Unix path inside a mounted handle.
# The OS-level analogue of @mirror/store/read; the path resolves
# through the lens to an oid, the oid resolves through the store
# to bytes. `imperfect` carries the bytes on success, opacity on
# failure (path does not resolve, oid drifted past verify, mount
# unbound).
read(p: ref) -> imperfect { \ }

# write(p, content) — write bytes at a Unix path. The lens
# computes the resulting oid, inserts into the store, and rewrites
# the mounted handle's namespace to point the path at the new oid.
# Returns the new handle's oid (the shard root after the write).
# Idempotent by content: writing the same bytes at the same path
# is a no-op at the store altitude.
write(p: ref, content: bytes) -> oid { \ }

# stat(p) — verdict on a Unix path within a mounted handle.
# `verdict` from @glass: pass | partial(confidence) |
# failure(reason). The OS calls this when it needs to know
# whether the path exists, what kind it is (file / directory),
# and what its size is. The lens answers from the oid-graph
# without materializing bytes.
stat(p: ref) -> verdict { \ }

out @mirror/lens/unix
out mount
out path
out read
out write
out stat
